WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:04.180
In August 2017, Hurricane Harvey made landfall

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:05.660
in southeast Texas,

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:08.100
dumping more than a meter and a half of water

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.800
on the Houston area in 4 days.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.440
It was the wettest hurricane in U.S. history,

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.140
causing extensive flooding and

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:18.380
125 billion dollars in damage.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:22.740
So, after the storm, where did all of that water go?

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.820
The earth’s crust is surprisingly elastic --

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:27.980
it actually sinks under the immense weight

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.900
of new water -- like what was released by Harvey.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.129
“So it kind of behaves like a mattress, in a way.

00:00:34.129 --> 00:00:35.820
So if I was to sit down on a mattress,

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.400
I would depress that mattress

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.120
just because of my weight, right? And so

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:42.860
I can measure my own weight

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:45.620
based on how much I depress that mattress”

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:48.780
To track the water, researchers used GPS satellites

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.540
to measure fluctuations in elevation of the earth's crust

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:56.080
subsidence (down) and uplift (up).

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.640
“You could use that measuring stick

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:01.720
of the amount of depression

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.740
to try to figure out the weight of the water

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:06.920
The land around Houston sank as much as 20mm

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:09.260
during Hurricane Harvey, with a clear pattern

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:12.040
left in the crust that moved across the Gulf Coast

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:16.020
for seven days, consistent with Harvey’s position.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.640
"The GPS not only to figure out where that water is

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:19.860
and how much occurred, but then

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.880
allows us to track how it dissipates,

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.420
how quickly it dissipates and how much dissipates.”

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:27.320
In the eight days following Harvey’s landfall,

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:30.040
30% of the stormwater was stored on land

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:31.520
as standing water,

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.700
60% of the stormwater immediately

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:36.040
drained into the ocean and Galveston Bay,

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:39.540
and the remaining 10% was lost to evaporation.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.560
It took 5 full weeks for the water stored on land t

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:46.440
to dissipate through evaporation or groundwater runoff

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.700
“OK ,we can do it for Harvey, that’s easy,

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:50.480
because theres so much water, that weight

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.080
was so large, that depression in that mattress

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.120
is going to be really easy to see in the data.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.420
So the next step is to say what about other

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:00.340
smaller, more frequent hurricanes with less water?

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.660
Could we apply the same type of analysis?”

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:05.180
The ability to track daily changes in the movement of

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:08.380
water during extreme events can help flood managers

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:10.180
better prepare for storms,

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:13.740
which will strike with increased frequency and ferocity

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:15.120
as global warming causes the

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:17.520
oceans temperatures to rise.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.500
If we have any hope of weathering these future storms,

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.760
we must remember that, as my mom always says

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.220
“failure to plan is planning to fail”

